---
import ServiceSection from "../components/ServiceSection.astro";
import Endpoint from "../components/Endpoint.astro";
import RustSchema from "../components/RustSchema.astro";
---

<ServiceSection id="service-chat">
  <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat</code> — channels, roster, and messages</Fragment>
<p>The four-bit service slot selects the Chat service. The three-bit
    <code>channelIndex</code> selects a joined channel. A successful join returns
    this index. The client must store it for messages, roster updates, and leave
    records. The documented implementation permits six concurrent joined channels.</p>
    <p class="endpoint-index">
      <a href="#chat-bootstrap">bootstrap order</a>
      <a href="#chat-channel-list-query">Chat/21</a><a href="#chat-channel-list-result">Chat/22</a>
      <a href="#chat-category-query">Chat/23</a><a href="#chat-category-result">Chat/24</a>
      <a href="#chat-conference-query">Chat/25</a><a href="#chat-conference-result">Chat/26</a>
      <a href="#chat-join-request">Chat/0</a><a href="#chat-notify">Chat/1</a>
      <a href="#chat-leave-request">Chat/2</a><a href="#chat-message-send">Chat/11 send</a>
      <a href="#chat-message-recv">Chat/11 receive</a><a href="#chat-join-result">Chat/27</a>
      <a href="#chat-whisper-send">Chat/19 send</a><a href="#chat-whisper-recv">Chat/19 receive</a>
      <a href="#chat-whisper-echo">Chat/30</a><a href="#chat-whisper-undeliverable">Chat/20</a>
      <a href="#chat-invite-notify">Chat/4</a><a href="#chat-invite-answer">Chat/5 and Chat/6</a>
      <a href="#chat-status-change">Chat/9</a><a href="#chat-create-and-invite">Chat/10</a>
      <a href="#chat-datagram-update">Chat/13</a>
      <a href="#chat-modify-channel-list">Chat/32 and Chat/33</a>
    </p>

    <h4 id="chat-bootstrap">Chat readiness</h4>
    <p>Base97563 prepares chat by selecting a playable identity and loading the
    regional channel directory.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Request <a href="#cache-get-stream-items-request"><code>Cach/9</code></a>
      stream items <code>BNET/ERRS/enUS</code> and <code>BNET/CONF/enUS</code> with
      distinct request tokens.</li>
      <li>When <a href="#toon-list"><code>Toon/0</code></a> supplies the account's
      playable identities, choose one with <a href="#toon-select"><code>Toon/5</code></a>.
      Wait for <a href="#toon-selected"><code>Toon/6</code></a>.</li>
      <li>After <code>Toon/6</code>, send <code>Pres/2</code>. Then send
      <a href="#chat-conference-query"><code>Chat/25</code></a> and
      <a href="#chat-channel-list-query"><code>Chat/21</code></a>. Collect
      <code>Chat/26</code> pages until <code>isLast</code> is true, and wait for
      <code>Chat/22</code>.</li>
      <li>For a public channel, also wait for the cache response. Select the channel
      ID from the downloaded <code>BNET/CONF/enUS</code> catalog.</li>
      <li>Send <a href="#chat-join-request"><code>Chat/0</code></a> for the desired
      public, private, or club channel. A successful <a href="#chat-join-result"><code>Chat/27</code></a>
      assigns the <code>channelIndex</code> and local member handle.</li>
      <li>Apply <a href="#chat-notify"><code>Chat/1</code></a> membership batches
      until <code>endOfInitial</code> is true. The roster is then complete and the
      client can use the assigned channel index with <code>Chat/11</code> and
      <code>Chat/2</code>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>These steps define state dependencies. Other protected records can occur
    between them, and the client must dispatch each complete record.</p>

    <Endpoint id="chat-channel-list-query" direction="client → server" phase="protected discovery" structure="empty payload" status="live verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/21</code> Chat::ChannelListRequest</Fragment>
      <p>Requests the account's current channel-list descriptors.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct ChannelListRequest; // zero payload bits`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-category-query" direction="client → server" phase="protected discovery" structure="empty payload" status="capture verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/23</code> Chat::EnumCategoryDescriptions</Fragment>
      <p>Requests the category directory used to group public conferences.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct EnumCategoryDescriptions; // zero payload bits`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-category-result" direction="server → client" phase="protected discovery" structure="generated order" status="capture decoded">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/24</code> Chat::CategoryDescriptions</Fragment>
      <p>Returns one page of category descriptions. Each entry exposes its logical
      category ID, localized name ID, and sort order. The generated entry also
      contains internal state and a locator variant that are skipped by the current
      value decoder.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct CategoryDescriptions {
    entries: Vec<CategoryDescription>, // count: 6 wire bits
    is_last: bool,
}

struct CategoryDescription {
    id: u8,
    name: u16,
    sort_order: u16,
}`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-channel-list-result" direction="server → client" phase="protected discovery" structure="generated order" status="live verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/22</code> Chat::ChannelListResponse</Fragment>
      <p>Returns auto-join and recently joined channel descriptors.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct ChannelListResponse {
    _reserved: Reserved<37>, // generated runs: 27 + 1 + 9 bits
    entries: Vec<ChannelDescriptor>, // count: 6 wire bits
    identifier: u16,
}

struct ChannelDescriptor {
    kind: u8,
    index: u16,
    _reserved: Reserved<24>,
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note">The descriptor ID and public
      <code>ChannelNameStringId</code> occupy separate namespaces. Public joins use
      the ID from the regional cache catalog.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-conference-query" direction="client → server" phase="protected discovery" structure="empty payload" status="live verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/25</code> Chat::EnumConferenceDescriptions</Fragment>
      <p>Requests the regional public-conference catalog.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct EnumConferenceDescriptions; // zero payload bits`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-conference-result" direction="server → client" phase="protected discovery" structure="generated order" status="live verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/26</code> Chat::ConferenceDescriptions</Fragment>
      <p>Returns one page of public numeric conference descriptions.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct ConferenceDescriptions {
    is_last: bool,
    _reserved: Reserved<27>,
    decoy: Option<i32>,
    entries: Vec<ConferenceDescription>, // count: 6 wire bits
}

struct ConferenceDescription {
    _reserved: Reserved<23>,
    identifier: u32,
    sort_order: u16,
    marker: bool,
}`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-join-request" direction="client → server" phase="protected" structure="logical order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/0</code> Chat::JoinRequest2</Fragment>
      <p>Requests membership in a private, public, or club conference.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct JoinRequest {
    token: u32,
    key: ConferenceLocator,
}

enum ConferenceLocator {
    Private(String),                 // tag 0; 0..=31 chars, 0..=124 bytes
    Public { locale: FourCC, name: u16 }, // tag 2
    Club { _reserved: Reserved<16>, club_id: u32 }, // tag 3
}

// Selector tag 1 is reserved.`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note">Choice value 1 is reserved. Generated code writes
      the locator before the request token. The private arm uses a seven-bit UTF-8
      byte count. It then aligns to a byte and writes the name.</p>
      <p class="endpoint-note">The club arm writes 16 zero bits before the group
      ID. For live group 535241, the ID starts at bit 29. The request token starts
      at bit 61. Live data confirms public ID 1033 and a named private room.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-join-result" direction="server → client" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="live verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/27</code> Chat::JoinNotify2</Fragment>
      <p>Acknowledges or rejects a join and assigns the local channel index used by
      membership, message, and leave records.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct JoinNotify {
    result: JoinResult,
    token: Option<u32>, // generated order places this last
}

enum JoinResult {
    Success {
        member_handle: u32,
        channel_index: Bits<3>, // 0..=6
        voice_session_id: VoiceSessionId,
        channel_type: Bits<4>,
        name: Option<String>,
        config: Option<ChannelConfig>,
        inviter: Option<PlayerTarget>,
    },
    Failure { reason: u16, channel_type: Option<Bits<4>> },
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note">The generated decoder is result-first/token-last and
      includes reserved runs absent from reflection metadata. Correlate a response
      by token when the optional token is present. If it is omitted, fall back to
      the requested channel type. A successful public result may omit the redundant
      public channel name ID; when present, it must match the request.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-notify" direction="server → client" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="live verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/1</code> Chat notify family</Fragment>
      <p>Command 1 carries <code>MembershipChangeNotify</code>, which creates the
      initial roster and then applies incremental membership changes.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct MembershipChangeNotify {
    end_of_initial: bool,
    channel_index: Bits<3>,        // 0..=6
    changes: Vec<MembershipChange>, // count: 1..=64
}

enum MembershipChange {
    Leaving(LeavingMember),
    Joining {
        member_handle: u32,
        presence_id: u32,
        statuses: Vec<MemberStatus>,
    },
    UpdateStatus { member_handle: u32, status: MemberStatus },
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note"><code>endOfInitial</code> marks completion of the
      initial roster snapshot. Generated ToonName status values provide display
      names directly. After the initial snapshot, the same structure carries joins,
      leaves, and status changes.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-invite-notify" direction="server → client" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/4</code> Chat::InviteNotify</Fragment>
      <p>Announces an invitation to a party channel. The channel index is stored for
      the later accept or decline record.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct InviteNotify {
    channel_type: Bits<4>,
    inviter_presence: u32,
    channel_index: Bits<3>, // 0..=6
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note">The generated order is channel type, inviter
      presence, then channel index. The metadata member order is different.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-invite-answer" direction="client → server" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/5</code> InviteAccept and <code>Chat/6</code> InviteDecline</Fragment>
      <p>Answers a party-channel invitation. The selected command identifies the
      answer.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct InviteAnswer {
    channel_index: Bits<3>, // 0..=6
}`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-status-change" direction="client → server" phase="protected" structure="verified reflected order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/9</code> Chat::StatusChangeRequest</Fragment>
      <p>This record is sent after a party invitation is accepted. It marks the
      local member as online.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct StatusChangeRequest {
    channel_index: Bits<3>,
    member_handle: u32,
    status_change: StatusChange,
}

enum StatusChange {
    Party {
        party_status: u8,    // 1 = online
        expansion_level: u8, // 3
        captain: bool,       // false
    },
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note">The service-less acknowledgement uses the same
      six-bit command value. It carries a nine-bit result.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-create-and-invite" direction="client → server" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="capture decoded">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/10</code> Chat::CreateAndInviteRequest</Fragment>
      <p>Creates a party channel and invites one player target. The two tokens
      correlate the create and invite operations.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct CreateAndInviteRequest {
    create_token: u32,
    target: PlayerTarget,
    invite_token: u32,
    channel_type: ChannelType,
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note">The captured generated order places the target
      between the two tokens. A live party request targeted the player's full toon
      name and used channel type 3.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-datagram-update" direction="bidirectional" phase="protected" structure="verified reflected order" status="capture decoded">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/13</code> Chat::DatagramConnectionUpdate</Fragment>
      <p>Supplies a datagram target and connection information.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct DatagramConnectionUpdate {
    target: PlayerTarget,
    info: DatagramConnectionInfo,
}`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-modify-channel-list" direction="bidirectional" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="capture decoded">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/32</code> ModifyChannelList and <code>Chat/33</code> response</Fragment>
      <p>Updates the account's auto-join or recently joined channel list. Two
      request generations share command 32: one carries a public partial name, and
      the other carries a general conference locator.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`// client → server, Chat/32
struct ModifyChannelListRequest {
    token: u32,
    index: u8,
    name: String,
    list_type: ChannelListType,
}

struct ModifyChannelListRequest2 {
    token: u32,
    index: u8,
    key: ConferenceLocator,
    list_type: ChannelListType,
}

// server → client, Chat/33
struct ModifyChannelListResponse2 {
    token: u32,
    result: Result<(), u16>,
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note">The two request types require discriminator logic;
      a discriminator combines with route <code>Chat/32</code> to select the type.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-whisper-send" direction="client → server" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/19</code> Chat::WhisperSend</Fragment>
      <p>Sends a private message to one resolved Battle.net identity. The three-bit
      <code>PlayerTarget</code> choice selects the identity form. The body uses the
      same ten-bit length as <code>Chat/11</code>.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct WhisperSend {
    target: PlayerTarget, // selector: 3 wire bits
    body: String,         // byte count: 10 wire bits, 0..=1020; byte-aligned
}

enum PlayerTarget {
    LocalPresenceId { presence_id: u32 }, // tag 0
    ToonName {                            // tag 1
        region: u8,
        program_id: u32,
        realm: u32,
        name: String, // byte count: 7 wire bits, stored as len - 2
    },
    AccountId { account_id: u32 }, // tag 3
    ToonHandle {                   // tag 5
        program_id: u32,
        region: u8,
        realm: u32,
        id: u64,
    },
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note">The generated writer emits a ten-bit body count,
      deliberately bypassing the stale eight-bit reflection shape. The writer emits
      choice tags 0, 1, 3, and 5. The arm field order differs: <code>toonName</code>
      is region → program → realm, while <code>toonHandle</code> is program →
      region → realm → id. The retail vector <code>53050add6816012e…</code>
      confirms the choice-0 arm.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-whisper-recv" direction="server → client" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/19</code> Chat::WhisperRecv</Fragment>
      <p>Delivers an inbound whisper. The peer is carried as a full
      <code>ToonName</code> so the reply can target the sender's toon directly.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct WhisperRecv {
    whisper: Whisper,
    peer: ToonFullName,
    body: String, // byte count: 10 wire bits, 0..=1020; byte-aligned
}

struct ToonFullName {
    region: u8,
    program_id: u32,
    realm: u32,
    name: String, // byte count: 7 wire bits, stored as len - 2
}`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-whisper-echo" direction="server → client" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/30</code> Chat::WhisperEchoRecv</Fragment>
      <p>The server's echo of a whisper this client itself sent, so an outbound
      whisper appears in the conversation once acknowledged.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`// Same wire layout and decoder as Chat/19 WhisperRecv.
type WhisperEchoRecv = WhisperRecv;`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-whisper-undeliverable" direction="server → client" phase="protected" structure="unresolved" status="defined, decoding pending">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/20</code> Chat::WhisperUndeliverable</Fragment>
      <p>The whisper-undeliverable sibling of the whisper family. Route mapping,
      decoding, and generated-order recovery remain pending.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-leave-request" direction="client → server" phase="protected" structure="logical order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/2</code> Chat::LeaveRequest</Fragment>
      <p>Leaves one locally tracked chat room.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct LeaveRequest {
    channel_index: Bits<3>, // 0..=6
}`} />
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-message-send" direction="client → server" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/11</code> Chat::MessageSend</Fragment>
      <p>Sends a UTF-8 message to one joined room.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct MessageSend {
    body: String,                // byte count: 10 wire bits, 0..=1020; byte-aligned
    channel_index: Bits<3>,      // 0..=6
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note">Generated validation separately limits the body to
      255 Unicode characters. Reflection lists channel index before body.</p>
    </Endpoint>

    <Endpoint id="chat-message-recv" direction="server → client" phase="protected" structure="generated order" status="verified">
      <Fragment slot="title"><code>Chat/11</code> Chat::MessageRecv</Fragment>
      <p>Delivers a message with the sender's channel-local member handle.</p>
      <RustSchema code={`struct MessageRecv {
    member_handle: u32,
    body: String,           // byte count: 10 wire bits, 0..=1020; byte-aligned
    channel_index: Bits<3>, // 0..=6
}`} />
      <p class="endpoint-note">The 32-bit handle starts at the payload's current
      bit position and can cross byte boundaries in the complete record.</p>
    </Endpoint>

</ServiceSection>
